── SECTION 5: Bandwidth-to-Compute Balance ──────────────────────────

  The b:f ratio = memory bandwidth (GB/s) / AI peak FLOP/s (TFLOP/s)
  It tells you how many bytes of memory each TFLOP of compute can afford.
  Lower b:f means the GPU is more compute-heavy; bandwidth becomes the limit.

  GPU                      BW GB/s   AI TFLOP/s   b:f (bytes/FLOP)
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  GTX 580 (2012)               192            2             0.1200
  V100 (2017)                  900          112             0.0080
  A100 (2020)                2,000          312             0.0064
  H100 (2022)                3,350          989             0.0034
  B200 (2024)                8,000         4500             0.0018

  Your GPU (NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4060 Laptop GPU):
    FP32 estimate: ~9.2 TFLOP/s  |  VRAM: 9 GB
    For an exact b:f ratio, find your GPU's bandwidth spec and compute:
    b:f = bandwidth_GB_per_s / (fp16_tflops × 1000)

  Interpreting b:f ratio:
    > 0.20  bytes/FLOP → bandwidth-rich GPU (older, fewer Tensor Cores)
    0.05–0.20           → balanced; workload type determines bottleneck
    < 0.05  bytes/FLOP → compute-rich GPU; most workloads are memory-bound

  The H100 (0.0034 bytes/FLOP) is so compute-heavy that nearly every
  AI workload runs into the memory wall before the compute ceiling.
  This is why quantization and FlashAttention matter more, not less,
  on newer hardware.

── SECTION 6: Reflection Questions ──────────────────────────────────

  Think through these before moving to Chapter 1. No code needed — just reasoning.

  Q1. AlexNet trained for 6 days on two GTX 580s in 2012.
      If it ran today on two H100s, how long would it take?
      (Hint: compare FP16 TFLOP/s, then consider that AlexNet used FP32.)

  Q2. The H100 has 989 FP16 TFLOP/s but only 3,350 GB/s of bandwidth.
      The ridge point is 989,000 / 3,350 ≈ 295 FLOPs/byte.
      Most LLM decode operations have arithmetic intensity < 5 FLOPs/byte.
      What fraction of the H100's compute is LLM decode actually using?

  Q3. A colleague says: "We should upgrade from A100 to H100 — it's 3× faster."
      Under what conditions is this true? Under what conditions is it false?
      (Hint: think about which ceiling your workload is currently hitting.)

  Q4. Your model trains in FP32 and you are about to switch to BF16.
      Your GPU is an RTX 3090 (Ampere, compute capability 8.6).
      What speedup do you expect for: (a) a large GEMM, (b) a ReLU activation?
      Why are the answers different?

